begin model

#@title: CaMKII six-state trimer holoenzyme (Michalski and Loew, 2012)

#@description: |
#  Exact rule-based model of the six-state CaMKII holoenzyme of Michalski and
#  Loew (2012), written for a three-subunit ring. Each subunit occupies one of
#  the six physiologically relevant states of Fig. 1b: autoinhibited (Duu),
#  CaM-bound (Cu), CaM-bound and phospho-T286 (Cp), phospho-T286 alone (Dpu),
#  phospho-T286 with phospho-T305 (Dpp), and phospho-T305 alone (Dup, which is
#  completely inert because T305 phosphorylation blocks CaM rebinding).
#  Calmodulin itself is resolved: CaM binds up to four calcium ions
#  sequentially, and only CaM4 binds CaMKII, with a higher on-rate and a much
#  slower off-rate once T286 is phosphorylated (calmodulin trapping). CaM
#  dissociation has two routes, direct release of CaM4 and a calcium-dependent
#  route that returns CaM2 to the pool, the second written as a Hill function
#  of calcium (Eq. 4). Intersubunit T286 autophosphorylation is unidirectional
#  around the ring and is driven at rate r1 by a Cu kinase, r2 by a Cp kinase,
#  and r3 by a CaM-free phospho-T286 kinase; T305 phosphorylation is
#  intrasubunit and fast once CaM has left, and a slow basal reaction
#  phosphorylates T305 in the autoinhibited state. PP1 removes either
#  phosphate through a shared Michaelis-Menten pool.
#
#  Fifteen rules generate the exact 83-species network of the trimer, with 487
#  unidirectional reactions or 413 counting each reversible pair once, matching
#  the species count of the published VCell model. Phospho-T286 rises
#  sigmoidally with calcium, while phospho-T305 is non-monotonic: it grows
#  with calcium because more CaM-bound CaMKII means more phospho-T286, then
#  falls at high calcium because trapped CaM shields T305. Trimers are as far
#  as exact enumeration can practically go for six states per subunit, which
#  is why the paper compares the infinite subunit holoenzyme approximation to
#  the trimer here.

#@keyword: |
#  signaling, synaptic plasticity, CaMKII, calmodulin, calcium, holoenzyme,
#  autophosphorylation, calmodulin trapping, protein phosphatase 1,
#  Michaelis-Menten, combinatorial complexity, cyclic complex, non-monotonic,
#  Rattus norvegicus

#@reference: |
#  Michalski PJ, Loew LM (2012). CaMKII activation and dynamics are independent
#  of the holoenzyme structure: an infinite subunit holoenzyme approximation.
#  Phys Biol 9:036010. doi:10.1088/1478-3975/9/3/036010

#@note: |
#  Corresponds to the public VCell model "BioNetGen CaMKII - Trimer - 6 states
#  - with phosphatase" (username pjmichal, biomodel key 61699798), whose 83
#  species this file reproduces. All rate constants are from Table S1 of the
#  supplementary material.
#
#  One discrepancy is worth recording. Sec. 2.2 of Michalski and Loew (2012)
#  states that both Dpu and Dpp phosphorylate Cu at rate r3, and the public
#  six-state ISHA model encodes exactly that. The public six-state trimer
#  instead admits only Dpu as an r3 kinase, which is why it has 407 reactions
#  against the 413 generated here. This file follows the paper text. The
#  difference is numerically negligible: over the whole Fig. 8 calcium range
#  the two readings differ by at most 0.12% relative in the phospho-T286
#  fraction and 0.04% in the phospho-T305 fraction, far below figure
#  resolution. The verification notebook reports the comparison.
#
#  Two of the eight combinations of the cam, T286 and T305 components are not
#  physiological: CaM cannot occupy the regulatory domain while T305 is
#  phosphorylated. The binding rules require T305~U, so those two combinations
#  are never generated and the network holds exactly six states per subunit.
#
#  Units are micromolar and seconds and ODE is the only simulation mode
#  provided; see the primary file for the unit and simulation-mode rationale.

begin parameters
  # Avogadro constant, retained for house-style population-conversion context.
  NA          6.02214076e23  # molecules/mol

  # Nominal reactor volume; not used by the concentration-based ODE protocol.
  V_ref       1e-15          # L/reactor

  # Number of subunits per holoenzyme; must match the ring in seed species.
  N_sub       3              # dimensionless

  # Sequential calcium binding to calmodulin, kon_i and koff_i of Eq. 1, from
  # Table S1 (originally Chiba et al. (2008)).
  kon_ca0     2.5            # /uM/s
  koff_ca0    50.0           # /s
  kon_ca1     88.25          # /uM/s
  koff_ca1    50.0           # /s
  kon_ca2     12.5           # /uM/s
  koff_ca2    1250.0         # /s
  kon_ca3     250.0          # /uM/s
  koff_ca3    1250.0         # /s

  # CaM4 binding to a subunit whose T286 is unphosphorylated. kon_u follows
  # from koff_u and the 66.7 nM dissociation constant of De Koninck and
  # Schulman (1998); koff_u2 and KCa_u are the amplitude and half-maximal
  # calcium of the two-step dissociation route of Eq. 4.
  kon_u       30.0           # /uM/s
  koff_u      2.0            # /s
  koff_u2     4.3            # /s
  KCa_u       0.94           # uM

  # The same constants for a subunit phosphorylated on T286. The 2000-fold
  # slower koff_p is calmodulin trapping.
  kon_p       10.0           # /uM/s
  koff_p      0.001          # /s
  koff_p2     0.08           # /s
  KCa_p       0.19           # uM

  # Intersubunit T286 autophosphorylation rates by a Cu, a Cp, and a CaM-free
  # phospho-T286 kinase. r3 = 0.8*r1 approximates the reduced activity of an
  # autonomous kinase toward exogenous substrates.
  r1          1.0            # /s
  r2          r1             # /s
  r3          0.8*r1         # /s

  # Intrasubunit T305 phosphorylation once CaM has left a phospho-T286 subunit,
  # and the slow basal T305 reaction of Colbran (1993).
  r305        10.0           # /s
  rbasal      5e-4           # /s

  # Michaelis-Menten constants for PP1 acting on phospho-T286 and phospho-T305,
  # measured by Bradshaw et al. (2003).
  kcat        1.72           # /s
  Km_PP1      11.0           # uM

  # Active phosphatase; zero for Figs. 8a and 8c, reset by the Fig. 8b/8d
  # protocol.
  PP1_tot     0.0            # uM

  # Protein concentrations used throughout Fig. 8.
  CaMKII_tot  0.005          # uM
  CaM_tot     1.0            # uM

  # Buffered calcium; scanned by the active protocols.
  Ca_uM       1.0            # uM
end parameters

begin molecule types
  # CaMKII subunit: cam is the regulatory-domain CaM4 site, T286 is the
  # intersubunit autophosphorylation site, T305 is the inhibitory site in the
  # CaM-binding domain, and cw/ccw are association-domain bonds to the
  # clockwise and counterclockwise neighbors in the ring.
  CaMKII(cam,T286~U~P,T305~U~P,cw,ccw)
  # Calmodulin; ca counts bound calcium ions and k is the CaMKII-binding site.
  CaM(ca~0~1~2~3~4,k)
  # Buffered calcium.
  Ca()
  # Protein phosphatase 1; acts through a Michaelis-Menten rate law and is
  # never covalently modified, so it carries no components.
  PP1()
end molecule types

begin seed species
  # Fully autoinhibited trimer. Subunit i holds subunit i+1 through cw, so a
  # kinase phosphorylates the neighbor bound to its cw component. Continuation
  # lines of a species graph cannot be indented, as the inserted whitespace
  # would split the graph.
  CaMKII(cam,T286~U,T305~U,cw!1,ccw!3).CaMKII(cam,T286~U,T305~U,cw!2,ccw!1).\
CaMKII(cam,T286~U,T305~U,cw!3,ccw!2) \
    CaMKII_tot/N_sub  # uM

  # All calmodulin starts calcium free and equilibrates with the buffered
  # calcium pool within milliseconds.
  CaM(ca~0,k)  CaM_tot  # uM

  # Buffered calcium and active phosphatase.
  $Ca()   Ca_uM    # uM
  $PP1()  PP1_tot  # uM
end seed species

begin observables
  # Total subunits; conserved and equal to CaMKII_tot.
  Molecules  Obs_Tot_CaMKII  CaMKII()

  # Phosphorylated subunits, the two ordinates of Fig. 8.
  Molecules  Obs_pT286       CaMKII(T286~P)
  Molecules  Obs_pT305       CaMKII(T305~P)

  # Active subunits: CaM bound, or phospho-T286 and therefore autonomous.
  Molecules  Obs_Active      CaMKII(cam!+,T286~U),CaMKII(T286~P)

  # The six subunit states of Fig. 1b, in the order Duu, Cu, Cp, Dpu, Dpp, Dup.
  Molecules  Obs_Duu         CaMKII(cam,T286~U,T305~U)
  Molecules  Obs_Cu          CaMKII(cam!+,T286~U)
  Molecules  Obs_Cp          CaMKII(cam!+,T286~P)
  Molecules  Obs_Dpu         CaMKII(cam,T286~P,T305~U)
  Molecules  Obs_Dpp         CaMKII(cam,T286~P,T305~P)
  Molecules  Obs_Dup         CaMKII(cam,T286~U,T305~P)

  # Free calmodulin pools and the buffered calcium and phosphatase levels.
  Molecules  Obs_Free_CaM4   CaM(ca~4,k)
  Molecules  Obs_Ca          Ca()
  Molecules  Obs_PP1         PP1()
end observables

begin functions
  # Calcium-dependent CaM dissociation from a subunit whose T286 is
  # unphosphorylated; the Hill term of Eq. 4 in Michalski and Loew (2012).
  koff_u_Ca() = koff_u2/(1 + (Obs_Ca/KCa_u)^2)

  # The same route for a phospho-T286 subunit.
  koff_p_Ca() = koff_p2/(1 + (Obs_Ca/KCa_p)^2)

  # Michaelis-Menten dephosphorylation. BioNetGen supplies the phospho-subunit
  # and PP1 factors, so only kcat/(Km + P) appears here; T286 and T305 share
  # one phosphatase pool, so the denominator carries both site totals.
  k_dephos() = kcat/(Km_PP1 + Obs_pT286 + Obs_pT305)

  # Fractions of total CaMKII, the ordinates of Figs. 8 and 9.
  Frac_pT286() = Obs_pT286/CaMKII_tot
  Frac_pT305() = Obs_pT305/CaMKII_tot
  Frac_active() = Obs_Active/CaMKII_tot
end functions

begin reaction rules
  # Sequential calcium binding to free calmodulin; CaM bound to CaMKII does not
  # exchange calcium, so these rules require an unbound k site.
  R_bind_Ca_0: CaM(ca~0,k) + Ca() <-> CaM(ca~1,k)  kon_ca0, koff_ca0
  R_bind_Ca_1: CaM(ca~1,k) + Ca() <-> CaM(ca~2,k)  kon_ca1, koff_ca1
  R_bind_Ca_2: CaM(ca~2,k) + Ca() <-> CaM(ca~3,k)  kon_ca2, koff_ca2
  R_bind_Ca_3: CaM(ca~3,k) + Ca() <-> CaM(ca~4,k)  kon_ca3, koff_ca3

  # Calcium-saturated CaM binds the regulatory domain of an autoinhibited
  # subunit. T305~U is required because phospho-T305 blocks CaM rebinding.
  R_bind_CaM4_u: \
    CaMKII(cam,T286~U,T305~U) + CaM(ca~4,k) <-> \
    CaMKII(cam!1,T286~U,T305~U).CaM(ca~4,k!1) \
    kon_u, koff_u

  # The same step for a phospho-T286 subunit; koff_p is 2000-fold slower.
  R_bind_CaM4_p: \
    CaMKII(cam,T286~P,T305~U) + CaM(ca~4,k) <-> \
    CaMKII(cam!1,T286~P,T305~U).CaM(ca~4,k!1) \
    kon_p, koff_p

  # Calcium-dependent CaM release: CaM4 loses two calcium ions and the
  # resulting CaM2 dissociates rapidly, so the pool receives CaM2 and 2 Ca.
  R_release_CaM_Ca_u: \
    CaMKII(cam!1,T286~U).CaM(ca~4,k!1) -> \
    CaMKII(cam,T286~U) + CaM(ca~2,k) + Ca() + Ca() \
    koff_u_Ca()

  R_release_CaM_Ca_p: \
    CaMKII(cam!1,T286~P).CaM(ca~4,k!1) -> \
    CaMKII(cam,T286~P) + CaM(ca~2,k) + Ca() + Ca() \
    koff_p_Ca()

  # Intersubunit T286 autophosphorylation of a CaM-bound neighbor by a
  # CaM-bound, unphosphorylated kinase.
  R_autophos_T286_by_Cu: \
    CaMKII(cam!+,T286~U,cw!1).CaMKII(ccw!1,cam!+,T286~U) -> \
    CaMKII(cam!+,T286~U,cw!1).CaMKII(ccw!1,cam!+,T286~P) \
    r1

  # The same reaction driven by a CaM-bound phospho-T286 kinase.
  R_autophos_T286_by_Cp: \
    CaMKII(cam!+,T286~P,cw!1).CaMKII(ccw!1,cam!+,T286~U) -> \
    CaMKII(cam!+,T286~P,cw!1).CaMKII(ccw!1,cam!+,T286~P) \
    r2

  # The same reaction driven by an autonomous CaM-free kinase, either Dpu or
  # Dpp, whose T305 state does not affect kinase activity.
  R_autophos_T286_by_D: \
    CaMKII(cam,T286~P,cw!1).CaMKII(ccw!1,cam!+,T286~U) -> \
    CaMKII(cam,T286~P,cw!1).CaMKII(ccw!1,cam!+,T286~P) \
    r3

  # Intrasubunit T305 phosphorylation, possible only once CaM has vacated the
  # CaM-binding domain of a phospho-T286 subunit.
  R_phos_T305: \
    CaMKII(cam,T286~P,T305~U) -> CaMKII(cam,T286~P,T305~P) \
    r305

  # Slow basal T305 autophosphorylation of the autoinhibited state, which
  # produces the inert Dup state.
  R_phos_T305_basal: \
    CaMKII(cam,T286~U,T305~U) -> CaMKII(cam,T286~U,T305~P) \
    rbasal

  # PP1 removes either phosphate with equal activity and no order preference.
  R_dephos_T286: \
    CaMKII(T286~P) + PP1() -> CaMKII(T286~U) + PP1() \
    k_dephos()

  R_dephos_T305: \
    CaMKII(T305~P) + PP1() -> CaMKII(T305~U) + PP1() \
    k_dephos()
end reaction rules

end model

begin actions
  generate_network({overwrite=>1})  # 83 species, 487 reactions
  saveConcentrations()

  # Ten-second phosphorylation reaction without phosphatase

  #@protocol: |
  #  Buffered calcium is scanned logarithmically from 0.3 to 100 uM with 5 nM
  #  total CaMKII and 1 uM total CaM and no phosphatase, and each point is
  #  integrated for 10 s. Frac_pT286 rises sigmoidally with calcium while
  #  Frac_pT305 peaks near 3 uM calcium and then falls, because trapped CaM
  #  shields T305 at high calcium.

  #@figure: Figs. 8a and 8c in Michalski and Loew (2012)

  resetConcentrations()
  parameter_scan({method=>"ode",parameter=>"Ca_uM",par_min=>0.3,par_max=>100,\
    n_scan_pts=>25,log_scale=>1,t_start=>0,t_end=>10,n_steps=>10,\
    suffix=>"scan",atol=>1e-14,rtol=>1e-10,print_functions=>1})

  # Equilibrium with 1 uM phosphatase

  #@protocol: |
  #  The same calcium scan with 1 uM PP1, integrated to 200 s so that every
  #  point reaches equilibrium. Both phospho-site fractions drop, the
  #  non-monotonic shape of Frac_pT305 survives, and these are the curves the
  #  infinite subunit holoenzyme approximation is compared against. Setting
  #  PP1_tot here is permanent for the remainder of the actions block.

  #@figure: Figs. 8b and 8d in Michalski and Loew (2012)

  resetConcentrations()
  setParameter("PP1_tot",1.0)
  parameter_scan({method=>"ode",parameter=>"Ca_uM",par_min=>0.3,par_max=>100,\
    n_scan_pts=>25,log_scale=>1,t_start=>0,t_end=>200,n_steps=>20,\
    suffix=>"scan_pp1",atol=>1e-14,rtol=>1e-10,print_functions=>1})
end actions
